'\" t
.TH GCP 1 "2026-11-15" "gcp 1.0" "User Commands"
.nh
.ds AD l
.SH NAME
gcp \- stage, commit and push all changes in one step, with safety checks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gcp
.RB [ \-y | \-\-yes ]
.RB [ \-i | \-\-interactive ]
.RB \(dq \fItype\fR[\fB(\fIscope\fB)\fR][\fB!\fR]\fB:\fR
.RI [ gitmoji ]
.IR description \(dq
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RI [ path ...]
.br
.B gcp amend
.RB [ \-y ]
.RB [ \-i ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR message ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RI [ path ...]
.br
.B gcp undo
.RB [ \-y ]
.br
.B gcp status
.br
.B gcp history
.RI [ n ]
.RI [ type ]
.br
.B gcp new
.I type name
.br
.B gcp sync
.RB [ \-y ]
.br
.B gcp pr
.RB [ \-y ]
.RB [ \-\-draft | \-\-merge ]
.br
.B gcp changelog
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.RI [ since ]
.br
.B gcp release
.RB [ \-y ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ major | minor | patch ]
.br
.B gcp
.RB \-h | \-\-help | \-v | \-V | \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gcp
stages all changes in the current repository (or only the given
.IR path s),
runs safety checks, commits
them with a Conventional Commits message and pushes the branch.
On
.BI release/ X . Y
branches it also creates and pushes a patch version tag for
.B fix
commits.
.PP
Before anything is staged,
.B gcp
shows the branch, remote, final commit message and the list of changed
files, and asks for confirmation.
.PP
The message must be passed as exactly one quoted argument, before any paths.
.PP
If one or more
.I path
arguments are given, only those files and directories are staged, checked
and committed. Other changes, staged or not, stay untouched. Each path must
exist or be tracked by git (a deleted tracked file is allowed).
A
.B \-\-
before the paths is optional and allows paths starting with a dash.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B amend \fR[\fB\-m\fR \fImessage\fR] [\fIpath\fR...]
Adds changes to the last commit and pushes it, for a forgotten file or a
typo. Without paths all changes are added, with paths only those, with
.B \-i
the files are picked from a list.
.B \-m
replaces the message (validated and given a gitmoji like a normal commit);
without changes, only the message is changed.
The same safety checks as for a normal commit apply.
Only allowed if the last commit is not pushed yet, because amending a
pushed commit would rewrite shared history. Merge commits are refused.
.TP
.B undo
Undoes the last commit.
If it is not pushed yet, it is removed and its changes stay staged
.RB ( "git reset \-\-soft" ).
If it is already pushed, a
.B revert
commit that reverses its changes is created and pushed, so the shared
history stays intact. Merge commits are refused.
Whether a commit is pushed is decided from the remote-tracking branches,
as of the last fetch.
.PP
The following two subcommands only show information and change nothing.
.TP
.B status
Shows the branch, its upstream and how many commits it is ahead or behind
(as of the last fetch), the changed files, and warnings about everything
.B gcp
would block: files that look like secrets, files of 100 MiB or more,
.B main
without
.BR ALLOW_MAIN ,
a missing remote, a rebase or merge in progress, a missing gitleaks.
On a
.BI release/ X . Y
branch it also shows which tag the next
.B fix
would create.
.TP
.B history \fR[\fIn\fR] [\fItype\fR]
Shows the last
.I n
commits (default 10), one per line with hash, age, message and tags.
With a
.I type
(for example
.BR fix ),
only commits of that type are shown;
.B breaking
shows breaking changes.
.SH BRANCH WORKFLOW
These subcommands cover the way from a new branch to a release:
.B gcp new
\(-> commits with
.B gcp
\(->
.B gcp pr
\(->
.BR "gcp release" .
The base branch is
.IB remote /HEAD
if known, otherwise
.BR main ,
otherwise
.BR master .
The remote is
.B origin
or the only configured one.
.TP
.B new \fItype name\fR...
Fetches the base branch and creates
.IB type / name
from the remote state, without an upstream, so the first
.B gcp
commit pushes the new branch.
The name is lowercased and spaces become dashes:
.B gcp new feat Login Form
creates
.BR feat/login-form .
Uncommitted changes are taken along.
.TP
.B sync \fR[\fB\-y\fR]
Brings the current branch up to date with the base branch: first pulls
commits pushed to the branch itself, then rebases it onto the latest base
.RB ( \-\-autostash ).
If commits of the branch are already pushed, the rebase gives them new
hashes; after a confirmation the branch is pushed with
.BR \-\-force\-with\-lease .
On the base branch itself,
.B sync
just pulls. On a conflict the rebase stops and
.B gcp
explains how to continue or abort.
.TP
.B pr \fR[\fB\-y\fR] [\fB\-\-draft\fR | \fB\-\-merge\fR]
Pushes the branch and creates a pull request against the base branch with
.BR gh (1).
With one commit, its message is the title; with several, the title is
built from the branch name
.RB ( "feat/login-form" " \(-> " "feat: ✨ login form" ),
so a squash merge again gives a Conventional Commit.
The body lists the commits. If a pull request is already open, the
branch is only pushed.
.B \-\-draft
creates a draft.
.B \-\-merge
squash merges the pull request right away, deletes the branch, switches
to the base branch and updates it.
.TP
.B changelog \fR[\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fIsince\fR]
Prints a Markdown changelog of the commits since the last
.BI v X . Y . Z
tag (or since
.IR since ,
a tag or commit), grouped into breaking changes, features, bug fixes and
performance. Gitmoji are removed, scopes are shown in bold.
.B \-\-all
adds the other types (docs, refactor, ...) and non-conventional commits.
Redirect it to a file to keep it, e.g.
.BR "gcp changelog > CHANGES.md" .
.TP
.B release \fR[\fB\-y\fR] [\fB\-n\fR] [\fBmajor\fR|\fBminor\fR|\fBpatch\fR]
Tags the next version on the base branch and pushes the tag.
The version follows Semantic Versioning from the commits since the last
tag: a breaking change bumps major (minor while still 0.x), a
.B feat
bumps minor, everything else patch. The first release is v0.1.0.
.BR major ", " minor " or " patch
overrides this.
The branch must be clean, pushed and up to date.
With
.BR gh (1)
and a GitHub remote, a GitHub release with the changelog as notes is
created too.
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
shows version and notes without tagging.
.B release
does not change files; if the project stores its version in a file,
commit that change first.
.SH COMMIT MESSAGE
The header must follow Conventional Commits 1.0.0:
.PP
.RS
.IB type ( scope )!: " description"
.RE
.PP
Allowed types:
.BR feat ", " fix ", " build ", " chore ", " ci ", " docs ", " style ,
.BR refactor ", " perf ", " test ", " revert .
The scope and the
.B !
are optional. The header may be at most 72 characters long.
.PP
A breaking change is marked either with
.B !
before the colon or with a
.B BREAKING CHANGE:
footer in the message body.
.SH GITMOJI
A gitmoji is inserted after the colon, based on the type.
The commit gets the unicode emoji, shown here by its shortcode:
.PP
.RS
.TS
l l.
feat	:sparkles:
fix	:bug:
docs	:memo:
style	:art:
refactor	:recycle:
perf	:zap:
test	:white_check_mark:
build	:package:
ci	:construction_worker:
chore	:wrench:
revert	:rewind:
breaking	:boom:
.TE
.RE
.PP
If the description already starts with a gitmoji, as unicode or as
.I :shortcode:
(for example
.BR :bug: ),
it is kept as is.
.SH SAFETY CHECKS
.B gcp
refuses to run, or stops before committing, when:
.IP \(bu 2
the message is not valid Conventional Commits, or the header is too long
.IP \(bu 2
a rebase, merge or cherry-pick is in progress
.IP \(bu 2
HEAD is detached
.IP \(bu 2
the branch is
.B main
or
.B master
(see
.BR ALLOW_MAIN )
.IP \(bu 2
there is no remote, or several remotes and no upstream
.IP \(bu 2
stdin is not an interactive terminal and
.B \-y
is not given
.IP \(bu 2
staged files look like secrets by name
.RB ( .env ", " id_rsa ", " *.pem ", " *.key ", " *.p12 ", " *.pfx ,
.BR credentials.json ).
Files ending in
.BR .example ", " .sample ", " .template " or " .dist
are allowed, and deleting such files is always allowed
.IP \(bu 2
.BR gitleaks (1)
finds secrets in the staged content
.IP \(bu 2
a staged file is 100 MiB or larger (GitHub rejects such files)
.IP \(bu 2
a commit hook rejects the commit
.PP
Whenever a check fails after staging, all changes are unstaged again.
Nothing in the working tree is lost.
.SH PUSH
If the branch has an upstream,
.B gcp
runs
.B git pull \-\-rebase \-\-autostash
before pushing. Uncommitted changes outside the committed paths are stashed
and restored automatically. On a conflict it stops and prints how to continue
.RB ( "git rebase \-\-continue" )
or undo
.RB ( "git rebase \-\-abort" ).
The commit itself is never lost.
.PP
If the branch has no upstream yet, it is pushed with
.BR "git push \-u" .
.SH RELEASE TAGS
Tags are only created on branches named
.BI release/ X . Y
(for example
.BR release/1.4 ).
Other names under
.B release/
are rejected.
.TP
.B fix
creates the next patch tag, for example
.B v1.4.3
after
.BR v1.4.2 ,
and pushes it. The tag is created after the rebase and push, so it always
points to the commit that is on the remote.
.TP
.BR feat " or breaking change"
rejected. New features need a new minor release branch, breaking changes a
new major release branch.
.TP
all other types
committed and pushed without a tag.
.PP
The first tag of a release line
.RB ( v \fIX\fB.\fIY\fB.0 )
must be created manually when the release branch is created. Without it,
.B gcp
stops before committing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Pick the files to commit from a list of all changed files.
With
.BR fzf (1)
installed, a fuzzy finder with a diff preview opens:
.B TAB
selects,
.B ctrl\-a
toggles all,
.B ENTER
confirms,
.B ESC
aborts.
Without fzf, a numbered list is shown and the selection is typed in,
for example
.B 1 3 5\-7
or
.B a
for all.
For a renamed file the old path is committed too.
Cannot be combined with
.I path
arguments. Needs an interactive terminal.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt. Needed when no interactive terminal is
available (scripts, editors, AI agents). The summary is still printed.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short overview of options, types and examples.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GCP_NO_FZF=1
Use the numbered list for
.B \-i
even if fzf is installed.
.TP
.B GCP_YES=1
Same as
.BR \-y .
.TP
.B ALLOW_MAIN=1
Allow committing directly to
.B main
or
.BR master .
.TP
.B SKIP_GITLEAKS=1
Skip the content secret scan. Not recommended.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, nothing to commit, or aborted by the user.
.TP
.B 1
A check failed or a git command failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
From a new branch to a release:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ gcp new feat login form
$ gcp "feat: add login form"
$ gcp pr \-\-merge
$ gcp release
.fi
.RE
.PP
Add a forgotten file to the last commit, then undo a commit:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ gcp amend forgotten.txt
$ gcp undo
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check before committing, look at the last fixes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ gcp status
$ gcp history 20 fix
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pick the files interactively:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ gcp \-i "fix: correct typos"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Commit only some files:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ gcp "docs: update install notes" README.md docs/
.fi
.RE
.PP
Fix on a feature branch:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ gcp "fix(api): handle empty response"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Commit message becomes
.B "fix(api): \(lqbug emoji\(rq handle empty response"
(the emoji for
.BR :bug: ).
.PP
Breaking change with footer:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ gcp "refactor: new auth flow

BREAKING CHANGE: login endpoint moved to /v2/login"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Patch release on a release branch:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ git switch release/1.4
$ gcp "fix: correct timezone in export"
\&...
Released v1.4.3
.fi
.RE
.SH REQUIREMENTS
bash >= 4, git >= 2.22,
.BR gitleaks (1)
>= 8.19.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-commit (1),
.BR git-push (1),
.BR git-tag (1),
.BR gitleaks (1)
.PP
